  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "pVjrGgZ1RhTas3ERPVlmua+ZBQs=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:54.087Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Open Social builds community platforms on Drupal and Mautic, and the research records its clients as institutions: UNDP, IATA and EU bodies. Those organisations govern membership by written rule, which makes a governance decision consequential rather than incidental.\n\nThe mechanism gap is Drupal's. Membership and moderation decisions land as node revisions \u2014 a diff of content with a timestamp and an editor. A revision cannot assert that a member was admitted or removed, by whom in which role, or on what grounds. At best the decision is inferable from the diff, and inference is not a record.\n\nWhat lifts this above a generic Drupal observation is the client list. Institutional bodies are accountable for how they treat their members and are periodically asked to demonstrate it, so an attributed governance event is a requirement they already recognise rather than a capability that has to be explained to them first.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- Open Social (NL Amsterdam) \u2014 Taco Potze, CEO; Bram ten Hove, CTO \u00b7 sales@getopensocial.com \u00b7 https://getopensocial.com\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> Built on Drupal and Mautic for community platforms serving UNDP, IATA and EU bodies. Membership and moderation decisions are node revisions, not attributed business events.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:54.087103Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:54.087109Z"
    }
  },
